# VS Code: install the "REST Client" extension (humao.rest-client), then click "Send Request" above each block.
# "# @name create" lets later requests reuse the created id via {{create.response.body.id}}.
# The same calls are in postman/interview-template.postman_collection.json and as curl lines in README.md.

### Health (actuator)
GET http://localhost:8080/actuator/health

### Create a task as user u1 (201 + Location header)
# @name create
POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks
Content-Type: application/json
X-User-Id: u1

{
  "title": "Write the interview template",
  "description": "Spring Boot, in-memory store, RestClient",
  "priority": "HIGH"
}

### Missing user header (400)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "no header" }

### Validation failure (400 with fieldErrors)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks
Content-Type: application/json
X-User-Id: u1

{
  "title": "   "
}

### Malformed JSON (400)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks
Content-Type: application/json
X-User-Id: u1

{ "title": 

### Wrong content type (415)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
X-User-Id: u1

title=form

### Get by id (200)
GET http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks/{{create.response.body.id}}

### Unknown id (404)
GET http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks/does-not-exist

### List all
GET http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks

### List one owner's tasks
GET http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks?ownerId=u1

### Complete as the owner (200, status DONE, outbound notification to /internal/echo)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks/{{create.response.body.id}}/complete
X-User-Id: u1

### Complete as someone else (403)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks/{{create.response.body.id}}/complete
X-User-Id: u2

### Complete again (409)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tasks/{{create.response.body.id}}/complete
X-User-Id: u1

### Echo endpoint directly (the stand-in downstream)
POST http://localhost:8080/internal/echo
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "hello": "world"
}
